{%- comment -%}
  Shared text-fidelity rules for the LLM render prompts (web rendering + every
  activity type). Included inside each prompt's system message. Targets the
  most common HTML-validation failures observed in production:
    - a leaf's leading list marker / label split out of its data-id element
      (produces both "Text node outside any data-id element" and a
      "Text mismatch" for the now-shortened data-id text),
    - provided text altered, shortened, or reordered,
    - whole text leaves dropped when prose is restructured into an activity,
    - standalone marker leaves ("1.", "(a)", "(iv)") dropped — common in
      matching activities where the model auto-numbers items instead,
    - invented visible text (instructions, headings, page numbers, self-added
      list markers) emitted as bare nodes with no data-id.
  These rules are ADDITIVE — they reinforce each prompt's own rules and never
  override the deliberate single-digit option-marker pattern some activities use.
{%- endcomment -%}
## PRESERVE THE PROVIDED TEXT (most validation failures come from breaking these)

Each text leaf in the content tree is shown as `role id=<data-id> "<text>"`. The quoted string is the COMPLETE text for that `data-id`.

1. Keep each leaf's quoted text WHOLE, inside its own single `data-id` element. When the text starts with a list marker or label — a number (`"1."`, `"10."`), a letter (`"a)"`), a roman numeral (`"(i)"`), or a prefix word (`"Mfano:"`, `"Kumbuka:"`, `"Wazo kuu:"`, `"Kipengele 1"`) — that marker is PART of the text. Render the whole string, marker included, inside that one `data-id` element. NEVER peel the leading marker off into a separate span/badge, and never drop it. (This is different from adding a purely decorative number that is not part of any provided leaf.)
2. Reproduce each leaf's text EXACTLY — do not translate, reorder, shorten, or split it across multiple `data-id` elements. You may still insert `[[blank:item-N]]` markers where an activity requires a blank.
3. Emit EVERY provided `data-id` exactly once. Even when restructuring prose into an activity, do not drop a text leaf: if it does not fit the layout, still render it (for example as an intro line, heading, or caption) so its `data-id` is present.
4. Some leaves ARE just a marker — their whole text is an enumeration marker or short label (`"1."`, `"10."`, `"(a)"`, `"(iv)"`, `"Mfano:"`). Give each its own `data-id` element too, rendered as the visible marker/label for its row, option, or matched item. NEVER drop such a leaf, and never replace it with your own auto-generated numbering — if a matching or numbered list already provides `"1."`, `"2."`, `"(a)"` leaves, those are the numbers you must show.
5. Do NOT add visible text that was not provided as a leaf — no invented instructions, headings, labels, page numbers, or multi-character list markers (e.g. `"iv"`). Render only the provided leaves. If you add a purely decorative marker or separator of your own, wrap it in an `aria-hidden="true"` element so it is not treated as content.

## DOWNSTREAM DISCOVERABILITY + INCLUSIVE ACCESS

- Every provided text or image leaf is a durable pipeline entity. Keep its exact `data-id` on the visible semantic element and NEVER place that element (or any ancestor) inside `aria-hidden="true"`, `inert`, `hidden`, `display:none`, or `visibility:hidden`. Image Captions, Language, Speech/Voice, editing, and validation must be able to locate the same entity later.
- Use native semantics that describe the content: real headings in hierarchy, paragraphs, lists/list items, tables with headers, and `<figure>`/`<figcaption>` for meaningful visuals. Keep DOM order equal to reading order. A visually hidden (`sr-only`) duplicate is allowed only when the same words are already visibly legible inside an image; its `data-id` must still remain available to screen readers and downstream stages.
- Do NOT add `tabindex="0"` to ordinary words, paragraphs, headings, captions, or static images. Static content is accessible through the screen-reader reading cursor and its `data-id`; making every word a keyboard tab stop would obstruct navigation. Native controls and links are keyboard-focusable automatically. Custom interactive regions, diagram hotspots, and activity controls MUST be focusable, have an accessible name, visible focus styling, and a keyboard-operable equivalent.

## MEANINGFUL IMAGES ON SMALL SCREENS

- A meaningful photo, illustration, chart, map, or diagram must remain large enough to understand on a phone. At `max-sm:` stack image/text columns and galleries into one column and give each meaningful image the full available reading width (`max-sm:w-full max-sm:max-w-none h-auto`). Do not preserve desktop thumbnail widths, fixed pixel widths, `max-w-xs`/`max-w-sm`, or multi-column grids that shrink the visual on mobile.
- Match the source prominence: if the original visual occupies most of its column/page region, it should occupy most of the mobile reading column too. As a review floor, meaningful standalone visuals should normally use at least about 80% of the available content width on mobile; genuine inline icons, logos, bullets, and small decorations are exempt.
- Preserve intrinsic aspect ratio (`h-auto`, `object-contain`; never stretch or crop away instructional content). For dense diagrams or images with important small text, use the full mobile width and provide the semantic caption, non-empty alternative, and structured labels/data; responsive reflow must not make the learner rely on pinch-zoom to identify the intended content.
